operator, except that it always returns a normal vector or matrix and not a
timeseries.

\strong{Vector timeseries}

A vector timeseries is  a univariate timeseries for which the underlying
data is a vector (and not a matrix). A vector timeseries only has one
dimension and only argument \code{i} is relevant.

If \code{i} is a numeric vector, the result is a vector (not a timeseries)
with the observations at the specified indices.
If \code{i} is a \code{period}, \code{period_range}, or a character that can
be coerced to a \code{period} or \code{period_range},  the result is a timeseries
with a period equal to the specified period (range). The result is padded
with NA values if the specified period or period range lies outside the
period range of the input timeseries.

The result of the \code{[[} operator is always a vector.

\strong{Matrix timeseries}

For a matrix timeseries the underlying data is a matrix, usually with
column  names. The first selection argument \code{i} can be used to
select rows or a period range, similarly as argument \code{i} is used the
select elements of vector timeseries.
If \code{i} is a numerical vector, the result is a normal matrix (not a
timeseries), if \code{i} is a \code{period} or \code{period_range} the result is a
timeseries.

For matrix timeseries, argument \code{j} can be used to select columns.
When used in combination with the \verb{<-} operator I can be used to add
columns. If \code{j} has length 1, the result is a vector timeseries
unless \code{drop = FALSE}.
The result of the \code{[[} operator is always a plain matrix (not a timeseries).
